    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How long does it take to remove a mechanics lien after filing a release?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Once you record the release with the county recorder, it typically takes 1–5 business days to appear in the county's online system. Title companies and lenders generally need to see it in the official record before they'll proceed with a closing or refinance. Some counties offer expedited recording for an additional fee."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can I release a lien without an attorney?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes. The release form itself is straightforward, and most states don't require attorney involvement. That said, if there's any dispute about the amount owed, any unusual language in the settlement agreement, or any ambiguity about what rights you're giving up, a construction attorney review is worth the cost."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What's the difference between a lien release and a lien satisfaction?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "In most contexts, these terms are used interchangeably. A 'satisfaction' historically referred to a mortgage being paid off, but in construction law, both terms refer to the same thing: a recorded document that cancels the lien."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What if the contractor refuses to release the lien after I paid them?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Each state provides a remedy. In California, you petition the court under Civil Code §8490. In Texas, the contractor faces statutory penalties up to $10,000. In Michigan, you have a damages claim. File a written demand first, document everything, and if the contractor doesn't respond within the statutory period, consult a construction attorney about a court-ordered release."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does a lien release affect the underlying contract dispute?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "It can, depending on the language. A narrowly drafted release that releases only the specific recorded lien should not affect other claims. But a broadly worded release that says 'releases all claims' could waive contract claims, warranty claims, or bond claims. Read the language before signing."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can I file a new lien after releasing one?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes — if you're still within the original lien filing deadline and have additional unpaid amounts. But if you released the lien and the lien deadline has passed, you cannot refile. Never release a lien as part of a partial settlement unless you're certain you're within the deadline window for any remaining amount."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is a notary required for a lien release?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "In California, Michigan, Texas, and Florida: yes. The county recorder will not record a document that isn't notarized. Bring a valid, government-issued photo ID to the notary."
      }
    }
